{"id":"cmpl-geulpu-2","object":"text_completion","created":1791991896,"model":"DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B-Q8_0","choices":[{"index":0,"text":"\nOkay, so I need to find the remainder when 14^102 is divided by 7. Hmm, let me think about how to approach this. I remember something about modular arithmetic from my math class, so maybe I can use that here.\n\nFirst, let me recall what modular arithmetic is. It's a system of arithmetic for integers where numbers wrap around after reaching a certain value, the modulus. In this case, the modulus is 7. So, when we talk about the remainder when dividing by 7, we're essentially working in modulo 7.\n\nNow, the problem is 14^102 divided by 7. I wonder if I can simplify 14 modulo 7 first. Let me calculate that. 14 divided by 7 is 2 with a remainder of 0, right? So, 14 is congruent to 0 modulo 7. That means 14 ≡ 0 mod 7.\n\nWait, if 14 is congruent to 0 mod 7, then 14^102 would be 0^102 mod 7. But 0 raised to any positive power is still 0. So, 0^102 is 0. Therefore, 14^102 ≡ 0 mod 7. That would mean the remainder is 0 when 14^102 is divided by 7.\n\nBut hold on, let me make sure I didn't make a mistake here. Sometimes, when dealing with exponents, especially with modular arithmetic, there might be some patterns or cycles that I can exploit. Maybe I should check if there's another way to approach this problem to confirm my answer.\n\nAnother method I remember is Euler's theorem, which states that if two numbers are coprime, then a^φ(n) ≡ 1 mod n, where φ(n) is Euler's totient function. But in this case, 14 and 7 are not coprime because 7 is a factor of 14. So, Euler's theorem doesn't apply here. That means I can't use that method.\n\nAlternatively, I can think about the binomial theorem or perhaps look for patterns in the powers of 14 modulo 7. Let me try that.\n\nLet's compute some small powers of 14 modulo 7 and see if there's a pattern.\n\n14^1 mod 7: 14 mod 7 is 0.\n\n14^2 mod 7: 14*14 = 196. 196 divided by 7 is 28 with no remainder, so 196 mod 7 is 0.\n\n14^3 mod 7: 14*14*14 = 2744. 2744 divided by 7 is 392 with no remainder, so 2744 mod 7 is 0.\n\nWait a second, it seems like every power of 14 is congruent to 0 modulo 7. So, 14^102 mod 7 would also be 0. Therefore, the remainder is 0.\n\nBut just to be thorough, let me think about why this is happening. Since 14 is a multiple of 7, any power of 14 will also be a multiple of 7. Therefore, when you divide 14^102 by 7, it will divide evenly with no remainder. So, the remainder is indeed 0.\n\nI think that makes sense. So, whether I approach it by simplifying 14 modulo 7 first or by recognizing that 14 is a multiple of 7, I end up with the same conclusion. Therefore, I can be confident that the remainder is 0.\n\n**Final Answer**\nThe remainder when \\(14^{102}\\) is divided by 7 is \\boxed{0}.\n\n\nTo find the remainder when \\(14^{102}\\) is divided by 7, we can use modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note that \\(14 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}\\). This is because 14 divided by 7 leaves a remainder of 0.\n\nSince \\(14 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}\\), raising both sides to the power of 102 gives:\n\\[14^{102} \\equiv 0^{102} \\pmod{7}\\]\n\nSince \\(0^{102} = 0\\), we have:\n\\[14^{102} \\equiv 0 \\pmod{7}\\]\n\nThus, the remainder when \\(14^{102}\\) is divided by 7 is \\(\\boxed{0}\\).","logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":36,"completion_tokens":1009,"total_tokens":1045},"geulpu":{"seed":13006733313559584705,"tokens":[151648,198,32313,11,773,358,1184,311,1477,279,26313,979,220,16,19,61,16,15,17,374,17779,553,220,22,13,88190,11,1077,752,1744,911,1246,311,5486,419,13,358,6099,2494,911,43893,34784,504,847,6888,536,11,773,7196,358,646,990,429,1588,382,5338,11,1077,752,19091,1128,43893,34784,374,13,1084,594,264,1849,315,34784,369,25780,1380,5109,15061,2163,1283,18739,264,3654,897,11,279,74024,13,758,419,1142,11,279,74024,374,220,22,13,2055,11,979,582,3061,911,279,26313,979,49702,553,220,22,11,582,2299,15791,3238,304,59864,220,22,38